From 07ff6d3e781ea02557fa75b273e54381a3cfa3aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2026 23:36:52 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] mt-discovery-tenant-scope: in-memory store cross-tenant
 collapse fix
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three subfixes addressing the discovery-routing investigation that
landed under patch17. All three reduce reliance on the shared in-memory
provider map (c.Providers) which is keyed by provider NAME only,
collapsing all tenants' rows into one entry via last-write-wins.

Subfix A — boot loader filter
  framework/configstore/rdb.go::GetProvidersConfig
  When BIFROST_DISABLE_DEFAULT_TENANT_CONFIG=true AND the ctx carries
  no tenant id (boot loader at lib/config.go::loadProviders), add
  WHERE tenant_id <> 'default' to exclude default-tenant rows.

  Without this, boot loads every tenant's rows into the shared map.
  Multiple tenants with a provider called "qwen" → iteration-order-
  non-deterministic last-write wins → root runtime ends up running
  against an arbitrary tenant's URL / keys. Discovery fires against
  the wrong endpoint. Per-tenant runtimes already load via the
  multitenant.Manager TenantLoader which IS tenant-scoped — so
  excluding default at boot just stops the root client from being
  polluted with cross-tenant config.

Subfix B1 — discovery hasNoKeys check via configstore, not in-memory
  transports/bifrost-http/server/server.go::ReloadProvider
  The hasNoKeys decision was reading s.Config.GetProviderKeysRaw(p)
  — the same shared in-memory map. After ANY tenant DELETEs a
  provider with the same name, the entry vanishes and the check
  falsely reports "no keys configured" for EVERY other tenant's
  subsequent discovery attempts, permanently skipping model discovery.

  Fixed by deriving hasNoKeys from the tenant-scoped
  s.Config.ConfigStore.GetKeysByProvider(ctx, p) lookup (the GORM
  scope callback adds WHERE tenant_id = ? when ctx carries the id),
  which is already loaded a few lines later for the allowed-models
  computation. One configstore round-trip now serves both purposes.

Subfix B2 — preserve tenant ctx into discovery's timeout context
  transports/bifrost-http/handlers/providers.go::attemptModelDiscovery
  Was: ctxWithTimeout, _ := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), ...)
       (drops the tenant id stamped by TenantResolverMiddleware)
  Now: derive the timeout ctx from a base ctx carrying the tenant id
       from TenantIDFromCtx(ctx) so ReloadProvider's downstream
       configstore lookups see the tenant via the GORM callback.

  This makes subfix B1's tenant-scoped lookup actually scope to the
  CALLING tenant, not "any qwen row".

Tests
  framework/configstore/rdb_provider_boot_filter_test.go locks in
  the four-quadrant truth table for the boot-time loader filter
  (env unset/set × ctx with/without tenant), plus the headline
  cross-tenant-collapse-avoided scenario.

  Subfix B1/B2's runtime behaviour is covered by the existing
  server-package integration tests and the live cluster smoke test:
  prior to this patch, fresh-seeded shaun's POST /providers/qwen/keys
  logged "model discovery skipped: no keys configured" even though
  the DB had the key (because globex's earlier DELETE wiped the
  shared in-memory qwen entry). After this patch, the configstore
  lookup sees the key correctly and discovery fires against vLLM.

Pairs with patch17 (mt-customprovider-and-strict-routing): patch17
fixed the catalog upsert filter to accept bare model IDs from custom
backends; patch18 fixes the discovery path that populates that
catalog, AND the boot loader that determines what the root runtime
sees.

Depends on patches 1, 2, 3, 4, 7 + patch17 (transitively — patch17
isn't a hard dependency but pairs naturally).

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
